WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.563
Meet SlimFaas! This CNCF Sandbox project
lets idle agent tools scale to zero for

00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:12.379
greener Kubernetes workloads. Keep your
existing HTTP app and call it directly, in

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:15.063
any language. No special driver needed!

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.053
First up: synchronous functions over HTTP.
Let's wake one up!

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.943
Find Fibonacci on the right. Zero ready
replicas. Its HTTP address is already

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:27.400
available.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.190
Send the call. See that dot? Kubernetes
starts a pod, SlimFaas forwards the

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.447
request, and the caller gets fifty-five.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.022
Now watch the idle timer. The last pod
goes to sleep, ready for the next call.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.046
Next up: asynchronous queues over HTTP.
Here comes the burst!

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:51.876
Thirty HTTP requests ask for Fibonacci of
forty-eight. All thirty get an accepted

00:00:51.876 --> 00:00:54.907
response while the work waits in the
queue.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.959
The backlog wakes more pods. Four workers
each take one calculation at a time,

00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:02.783
keeping CPU demand under control.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.845
The queue gives Kubernetes time to scale.
All thirty calculations finish. With the

00:01:08.845 --> 00:01:11.615
backlog empty, the workers return to zero.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.859
Publish and subscribe over HTTP: one event
for every replica!

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.935
Our agent system has two evaluators and
two audit pods. Each keeps its own tool

00:01:22.935 --> 00:01:24.989
catalog. A tool has just changed.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:31.540
Publish once. Follow those paths! All four
pods receive the HTTP event and discard

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.748
their stale copy.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.180
Now let's pass an agent result through a
durable Stream over HTTP.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:45.618
One HTTP append gives the evaluator and
the audit function their own copy to

00:01:45.618 --> 00:01:48.377
process. Each function tracks its own
progress.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.558
One replica per function handles this
attempt. Both subscriptions acknowledge

00:01:53.558 --> 00:01:59.519
sequence one, with zero lag. Retries are
possible, so make handlers safe to repeat.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:07.159
There's more! Submit a job over HTTP or by
cron. SlimFaas queues runs until a slot

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:11.959
opens. You can also schedule a function to
wake before the morning rush.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.061
Cache a tool result with Sets. Post a
value under a key over HTTP, set its

00:02:18.061 --> 00:02:20.964
expiry, then read it back on the next
call.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:28.171
With Files, stream bytes in over HTTP and
pass the key to the next agent. A few

00:02:28.171 --> 00:02:32.966
kilobytes or hundreds of megabytes? Same
route! Stream the artifact back before it

00:02:32.966 --> 00:02:33.335
expires.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.474
Give the local demo a spin and watch your
own HTTP tool wake up!
